<<~ ahu #how-to-read-this>>

! HOW TO READ THIS

Three weights stand apart throughout, and a claim never blurs them.

* ''MEASURED HERE'' — derived in this repository, against `syntaxes/`, the 65 bundled `tm-themes`,
  or the TiddlyWiki checkout beside it. The command stands beside the number.
* ''MEASURED ABROAD'' — a grammar fetched and TOKENIZED through `vscode-textmate`, or a doc fetched
  and grepped. The repository and path stand beside the quote.
* ''CLAIMED BY SOURCE'' — a vendor's own prose. Quoted, never paraphrased.

Twenty grammars answered: the VS Code bundles (markdown, html, TypeScript, TypeScriptReact, C++,
LaTeX, Python/MagicPython, Rust, Handlebars), Vue, AsciiDoc, Liquid, Jinja, Twig, LaTeX-Workshop,
and six wiki-lineage grammars (MediaWiki wikitext, Org, reStructuredText, DokuWiki, Creole, and
markdown again as the wiki-adjacent control).

The standing HOUSE RULING holds over everything below: ''this grammar names no language but its
own.'' Nothing here recommends borrowing a language's name to borrow its colour.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #q1-per-glyph>>

! 1 — PER-GLYPH DELIMITER TOKENS · OVERTURNED

''No canon speaks to it, and no grammar does it.'' Twenty grammars fetched and tokenized; not one
splits a multi-character delimiter into two sequentially-named tokens. This grammar splitting `[[`
into `punctuation.definition.link.outer.begin` then `punctuation.definition.link.inner.begin` stands
alone in the measured population.

!! What the references say

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — the TextMate 1.x manual's own `captures` worked example fuses a two-character
delimiter rather than splitting it: `match = '(@selector\()(.*?)(\))'` with capture 1 named
`storage.type.objc`, so //"the `storage.type.objc` name will only be assigned to `@selector(` and
`)`."// [[TextMate language grammars|https://macromates.com/manual/en/language_grammars]]

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — Sublime names delimiters as PAIRED, never per-character:
//"Punctuation at the beginning and end of strings should use: `punctuation.definition.string.begin`
/ `punctuation.definition.string.end`."//
[[Sublime scope naming|https://www.sublimetext.com/docs/scope_naming.html]]

MEASURED ABROAD — the VS Code syntax-highlight guide carries NO scope-naming guidance at all:
`grep -ic "naming"` reads 0 and `grep -ic "convention"` reads 0 over the fetched page. It defers to
TextMate twenty-five times and offers one punctuation example, single-character.

!! What the grammars do — MEASURED ABROAD, tokenized

|!grammar |!delimiter |!tokens |
|VS Code html |`</` |''ONE'' — `end: "(?i)(</)(\\2)\\s*(>)"`, capture 1 → `punctuation.definition.tag.begin.html` |
|VS Code TypeScript |`${` |ONE — `beginCaptures: {"0": {"name": "punctuation.definition.template-expression.begin.ts"}}` |
|VS Code markdown |`**` |ONE, both ends `punctuation.definition.bold.markdown` |
|VS Code markdown |```` ``` ```` |ONE, any fence length, `punctuation.definition.markdown` |
|Vue |`{{` `}}` |ONE each, `punctuation.definition.interpolation.{begin,end}.html.vue` |
|MediaWiki wikitext |`[[` `{{` |ONE each |
|DokuWiki |`[[` |ONE |
|Creole |`[[` |ONE |
|Org |`[[` |ONE |
|Liquid |`{%-` |ONE, ''three characters'' |
|Handlebars |`{{~{>` |ONE, arbitrary length — `begin: "(\\{\\{~?\\{*(>|!<)*)\\s*…"` |
|Twig |`{{-` |ONE, modifier included |

The one case that looks like two names on a sigil resolves as STACKING, not splitting:
LaTeX-Workshop gives the `\` of `\usepackage` both `keyword.control.preamble.latex` (capture 1,
spanning the whole word) and `punctuation.definition.function.latex` (capture 2, spanning the `\`
alone) — overlapping captures on one span.

Markdown's `](` reads as two tokens, and that misleads: `]` and `(` are two DIFFERENT GLYPHS closing
two different sections. Nothing in the corpus cuts one repeated glyph pair in half.

!! The split runs NARROWER than the council read — MEASURED HERE, tokenized

Taken through `tools/tokenizer.js` at `text.html.tiddlywiki5`:

* ''A call does NOT split.'' `<<` emits ONE token carrying a PAIRED name —
  `punctuation.definition.call.block.begin.tiddlywiki5 punctuation.definition.macrocallblock.begin.tiddlywiki5`
  — and `>>` the same. This grammar already writes the canonical idiom on its busiest delimiter.
* ''A widget does NOT split a delimiter either.'' `<` and `$` are two DIFFERENT GLYPHS, and the `$`
  carries a three-name stack — `entity.name.tag.html | entity.name.function.widget |
  punctuation.definition.tag.widget`. The closer `/>` emits ONE token. That reads as html's own
  shape, not as a cut delimiter.
* ''Exactly TWO constructs cut a repeated glyph pair in half'': the pretty link (`[[` → outer then
  inner, 7 tokens over 20 characters) and the image (`[` … `[`, 13 tokens over 28).

So the finding stands, and its population reads two rules rather than a habit.

!! What the house should take

''Fuse `[[` and `]]` to one token each.'' The distinction the split buys reaches machines only — both
halves resolve through `punctuation` today, MEASURED HERE — and it costs a reader nothing to lose
because no reader ever saw it. A consumer wanting the outer/inner boundary reads the span's WIDTH,
which the fused token still carries.

''Decline'' the reflex to keep the split "in case a theme rules deeper." Four bundled themes rule at
`punctuation.definition.link` (the catppuccin family) and three at `punctuation.definition`
(laserwave, nord, one-light) — MEASURED HERE — and every one of them reaches the FUSED name equally,
because neither `outer` nor `inner` sits in a selector any theme writes.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #q2-compound-root>>

! 2 — `string.punctuation.definition.*` · OVERTURNED, WITH A NAMED REPLACEMENT ALREADY IN THIS TREE

''An anti-pattern by both references and absent from every grammar measured — and the canonical
device that buys the same colour ships in this repository already, on headings.''

!! The reading, first

MEASURED HERE — the compound-root spelling stands at ''seven names across FOUR roots'', not one:

```
string.punctuation.definition.operand.{begin,end}.tiddlywiki5
variable.punctuation.definition.operand.{begin,end}.tiddlywiki5
entity.name.punctuation.definition.operand.{begin,end}.tiddlywiki5
markup.heading.punctuation.definition.tiddlywiki5
```

Fourteen occurrences of 1278 `name` values. The council reported one.

!! What canon says

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — Sublime rules against gratuitous nesting by name:
//"Avoid `entity.name.type.class` and `entity.name.type.struct` which unnecessarily nest scope labels
under `type`."//

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — and seats string delimiters under the PUNCTUATION root, explaining the filing
rule outright: //"The following scopes are punctuation scopes that are not embedded within other
scopes. For instance, the `string.` section includes documentation about scopes for string
punctuation."// The containment lives in the DOC's table of contents; the NAME keeps `punctuation`
at the root.

MEASURED ABROAD — `grep -o 'string\.punctuation[^"]*'` over thirteen flagship grammars returns
''zero''. So does `markup\.punctuation` and `keyword\.punctuation`. The only mid-segment
`punctuation` found anywhere reads incidental —
`comment.typehint.punctuation.notation.python`, `constant.character.escape.unicode.punctuation.rust`
— and neither colours a delimiter.

The two grammars that DO nest a delimiter under a content root both read as defects:
* ''Jinja'' — `"name": "variable.meta.scope.jinja"` with `"captures": [ { "name":
  "variable.entity.other.jinja.delimiter" } ]`. Two invented compound roots, and `captures` given as
  a JSON ARRAY rather than a number-keyed object, so those captures never fire at all.
* ''Creole'' — `invalid.illegal.punctuation.link.creole`, `punctuation` in the third segment, where
  no `punctuation.*` theme rule will ever reach it.

!! Why the house's OWN law already forbids it

`synthesis.mem` §F states the law: //"in a TextMate grammar the degradation path IS the dotted
prefix. Every invented segment must sit at the TAIL."// `entity.name.punctuation.definition.operand.begin`
degrades to `entity.name`, then `entity`, and NEVER to `punctuation.definition`. The compound root
does not add a family; it ''severs'' one.

!! The device canon actually uses — MEASURED ABROAD, first-party

VS Code's own bundled Python grammar stacks BOTH families on one delimiter token, twelve times:

```json
"3": { "name": "punctuation.definition.string.begin.python string.quoted.single.python" }
```

VS Code TypeScript does the same for a template backtick, and states the reason structurally —
`contentName` covers only the interior, so the backtick would otherwise lose the string colour:

```json
"2": { "name": "string.template.ts punctuation.definition.string.template.begin.ts" }
```

181 space-separated multi-scope `name` values stand across the fetched corpus (python 77, ts 42,
tsx 42, rust 9, latex 6, handlebars 5). `synthesis.mem` §E1 already names this move
DOUBLE-SCOPING and records four ecosystems that built a mechanism for it.

!! And it already ships here — on the heading

MEASURED HERE, `syntaxes/tiddlywiki5.json:4521`:

```json
"name": "punctuation.definition.heading.tiddlywiki5 markup.heading.punctuation.definition.tiddlywiki5"
```

The heading's marks carry an INTACT `punctuation.definition.heading` chain FIRST and the heading
family LAST. Nothing severs. MEASURED HERE across 65 themes: the paired name paints 55/65 and reads
the heading's own colour; `punctuation.definition.heading` alone paints 42/65.

''The distinction that settles it: a compound root stands safe as a COMPANION and fatal as a SOLE
name.'' The heading pairs. The six operand names stand alone.

!! The fix, measured — AND IT COSTS SOMETHING

MEASURED HERE. The compound root runs FOUR times, not once: each operand kind seats its brackets
under its own body root, and the design WORKS — the bracket reads its body's colour.

|!operand kind |!bracket today |!reads its body's colour |!after pairing with an intact `punctuation.definition` chain |
|string |`string.punctuation.definition.operand.*` |''63/65'' |62/65 |
|variable |`variable.punctuation.definition.operand.*` |''58/65'' |54/65 |
|indirect |`entity.name.punctuation.definition.operand.*` |''42/65'' |38/65 |

''The remedy is NOT free, and the reason is exact.'' Seven bundled themes rule at
`punctuation.definition` — laserwave, nord, one-light, and the four catppuccins — and in those a
restored punctuation chain is DEEPER than the bare `string`/`variable` selector and therefore WINS.
Pairing hands the delimiter back the punctuation colour in precisely the themes the compound root was
built to escape.

So the trade reads: ''give up the delimiter-joins-content reading in one to four themes per kind, and
gain a `punctuation.definition` surface a reader's own `editor.tokenColorCustomizations` can name.''

`corpus/legibility-floor.txt:27` already weighed a DIFFERENT question — whether widening moved any
LEGIBILITY PAIR — and found it moved none, so the misfiling stood reported rather than shipped. That
reading holds. What it did not weigh is the published surface, and what it did not see is that the
shape runs across four roots rather than the one `string.` name it names.

!! What the house should take and decline

* ''TAKE:'' the heading's spelling as the house template — an intact `punctuation.definition` chain
  FIRST, the content family LAST. MagicPython writes it in that exact order, and MEASURED HERE the
  order is load-bearing: content-last reads the body in 62/65, content-first in 38/65.
* ''TAKE:'' that the shape stands at SIX sole-compound names across THREE roots, not one. Whatever
  the house rules, it rules on six.
* ''DECLINE:'' any NEW compound-root name standing alone. Zero respected grammars carry one, both
  near-misses carry an accompanying defect, and the house's own degradation law forbids an invented
  segment above the tail.
* ''UNDECIDED, and honestly so:'' whether to convert the six that already stand. The conversion costs
  the delimiter-joins-content reading in up to four themes per kind and buys a published surface
  nobody has yet asked for. Settle it by asking whether a reader will ever write
  `punctuation.definition.operand` into their own settings — a question no measurement here answers.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #q3-named-argument>>

! 3 — THE NAMED ARGUMENT AND ITS `=` · SPLIT VERDICT — the NAME overturns, the `=` VINDICATES

''Canon assigns the argument NAME by the SURFACE a construct sits on, not by call-versus-definition —
and on that rule a TiddlyWiki call takes the widget's family, not the call's. Canon assigns the `=`
by whether the host records it as a CHOICE — and on that rule the two families the council wanted
unified stand correctly apart.''

!! The references

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — Sublime on `variable.parameter`: //"Parameters to a function or methods should
use the following scope. This may also be used for other parameter-like variables, such as receivers
or named return values in Go."//

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — Sublime on the entity exception: //"The `entity` scopes are generally assigned to
the names of data structures, types and other uniquely-identifiable constructs in code and markup.
The notable exceptions are `entity.name.tag` and `entity.other.attribute-name`, which are used in
HTML and XML tags."//

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — Sublime on the `=`: `keyword.operator.assignment` sits under //"Operators are
typically symbols, so the term `keyword` can seem somewhat contradictory."// And separately:
//"Separators such as commas and colons should use: `punctuation.separator`."//

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — the TextMate manual names `variable.parameter` for the DECLARATION side only:
//"parameter — when the variable is declared as the parameter."// It says nothing about a call site.

Both `variable.parameter` and `entity.other.attribute-name` stand in Sublime's Minimal Scope
Coverage set, so a theme reaching either is not the question.

!! What comparable grammars do — MEASURED ABROAD

|!grammar |!named-arg name |!the binder |
|Python `f(x=1)` |`variable.parameter.function-call.python` |`keyword.operator.assignment.python` |
|''MediaWiki'' `{{T\|name=v}}` |`entity.other.attribute-name.local-name.wikitext` |''`keyword.operator.equal.wikitext`'' |
|Handlebars `{{h key=v}}` |`variable.parameter.handlebars` ''stacked inside'' `entity.other.attribute-name.handlebars` |falls to the rule name |
|JSX/TSX |`entity.other.attribute-name.tsx` |`keyword.operator.assignment.tsx` |
|VS Code html |`entity.other.attribute-name.html` |`punctuation.separator.key-value.html` |
|Vue |`entity.other.attribute-name.html.vue` |`punctuation.separator.key-value.html.vue` |
|Liquid `key: v` |`entity.other.attribute-name.liquid` (colon FUSED into the token) |none |
|Twig hash |`variable.other.twig` |`:` → `punctuation.separator.key-value.twig`; `=` → `keyword.operator.assignment.twig` |
|Jinja |''none'' — falls through to `variable.other.jinja` |`keyword.operator.assignment.jinja` |

The rule that falls out, and it cuts across the call/definition axis entirely:
* On a TAG, TEMPLATE or MARKUP surface — `entity.other.attribute-name`.
* On a PROGRAMMING-LANGUAGE call surface — `variable.parameter.function-call`.
* For the binder: `keyword.operator.assignment` where the grammar reads `=` as an operator;
  `punctuation.separator.key-value` where the grammar reads it as structure.

''The closest analogue in the whole corpus is MediaWiki's template call'' — `{{Template|name=value}}`,
a named-argument invocation on a wiki surface, exactly what `<<call name="value">>` is. It takes
`entity.other.attribute-name` for the name and a KEYWORD OPERATOR for the `=`. That is the hybrid,
and it is neither of the two rows the council set against each other.

!! The parse tree — MEASURED HERE, and it OVERTURNS the council's ground

The council read `parseMacroParameterAsAttribute` as the host refusing the distinction. Probed
against the checkout at `../TiddlyWiki5` (5.5.0-prerelease), the host makes THREE distinctions, and
one of them lands precisely on the row the council flagged.

```
<$list filter="a" xlink:href="b"/>
  -> {"filter":{"name":"filter","type":"string","value":"a"}, "xlink:href":{…}}

<<greeting name="world" tone:"warm">>
  -> {"name":{"name":"name","assignmentOperator":"=","type":"string","value":"world","quoted":true},
      "tone":{"name":"tone","assignmentOperator":":","type":"string","value":"warm","quoted":true}}

<<greeting positional>>
  -> {"0":{"type":"string","value":"positional","name":"0","isPositional":true}}
```

# ''A call's binder stands RECORDED IN THE TREE.'' `assignmentOperator` carries `"="` or `":"`. A
  widget attribute carries no such field, because a widget admits only `=` and the host has nothing
  to record. The call's `=` names a CHOICE the author made; the widget's names structure.
# ''A call admits a POSITIONAL parameter'' (`isPositional: true`, name `"0"`). A widget does not.
# ''The name regexps differ'' — `[^\/\s>"'`=:]+` for a call parameter, `[^\/\s>"'`=]+` for a widget
  attribute — so `xlink:href` parses as ONE widget attribute name and would cut at the colon in a
  call.

Two separate functions, `parseAttribute` at `parseutils.js:504` and `parseMacroParameterAsAttribute`
at `:323`. They agree on the node SHAPE and part on what the source may spell.

!! What the house should take and decline

* ''TAKE:'' unify the argument NAME on `entity.other.attribute-name`. The surface rule is the one
  canon writes, the wiki-lineage analogue writes it, and MEASURED HERE the current pair parts in 44
  of 65 themes and agrees in 6 — a reader meets one idea painted two ways. Handlebars' stack
  (`variable.parameter` inside `entity.other.attribute-name`) stands as the hedge where the precise
  name matters, and this house already ships that device.
* ''DECLINE the unification of the `=`.'' The council read `keyword.operator.assignment` at 65/65
  against `punctuation.separator.key-value.html` at 48/65 (both reproduced MEASURED HERE) as a reach
  accident to be collapsed. The parse tree says the two glyphs are not one thing: one carries a
  recorded operator value, the other carries none. Canon backs the split — Python and JSX and
  MediaWiki spell a chosen binder as an operator; html and Vue spell an obligatory one as a
  separator. ''The families are already right.''
* ''NOTE for the record:'' the call's binder may spell `:` as well as `=`, and the grammar naming it
  `keyword.operator.assignment` reads both correctly.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #q4-meta-vocabulary>>

! 4 — `meta.*`, NEVER STYLED, AND INTERPOLATED · VINDICATED

''Both references state plainly that `meta` carries no colour, and that is exactly what makes it the
safe home for an unbounded interpolated vocabulary. VS Code's own html grammar does the same thing
this grammar does, 79 times.''

!! Never styled — two independent authorities

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — TextMate manual: //"A minimal theme will only assign styles to 10 of the 11 root
groups below (`meta` does not get a visual style)"// and //"the entire line which declares a function
would be `meta.function` and the subsets would be `storage.type`, `entity.name.function`,
`variable.parameter` etc. and only the latter would be styled."//

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — Sublime: //"These are not intended to be styled by a color scheme, but used by
preferences and plugins."// and //"When styling scopes, resist the urge to directly style `meta`
scopes."//

So `meta.*` painting 0/65 reads as the CONTRACT HOLDING, never as 37% of the vocabulary wasted. The
council's F1 counted it as bloat; the canon counts it as correctness.

!! Interpolation — undocumented, and universally practised by the first party

MEASURED ABROAD — neither reference describes capture substitution in a scope name at all. The
TextMate manual documents `captures` and states //"Currently `name` is the only attribute
supported"//; its only `$` substitution anywhere is `include = "$self"`. Sublime's "interpolation"
hits all concern string interpolation in SOURCE CODE. The VS Code guide carries zero. ''Neither
blesses it and neither forbids it.''

MEASURED ABROAD — `grep -ohE '"name": "[^"]*\$[0-9][^"]*"'`:

|!grammar |!occurrences |!unique |
|VS Code html |''79'' |53 |
|Vue |56 |4 |
|VS Code LaTeX |1 |1 |
|VS Code markdown |1 |1 |
|ts · tsx · python · rust · handlebars · jinja · liquid · wikitext · twig |0 |0 |

52 of html's 53 unique interpolated names sit under `meta.`, and one of them is genuinely UNBOUNDED
— the capture is a pure exclusion class over any attribute name:

```json
"beginCaptures": { "0": { "name": "entity.other.attribute-name.html" } },
"end": "(?=\\s*+[^=\\s])",
"name": "meta.attribute.unrecognized.$1.html"
```

Vue interpolates under a STYLED root, which is the riskier move this house does not make:
`entity.name.tag.$1.html.vue`, capture class `[a-zA-Z0-9:-]+`.

!! The reading on our own vocabulary — MEASURED HERE

```
distinct scope names across syntaxes/ : 551
  meta 194 · punctuation 145 · markup 50 · keyword 40 · entity 33 · string 21 · source 21
  variable 16 · text 10 · constant 8 · comment 6 · invalid 4 · support 3
names carrying a capture interpolation : 54  (meta 49 · keyword 3 · constant 1 · markup 1)
```

Forty-nine of the 194 `meta` names are TEMPLATES, not realized names — and their shapes read
`meta.tag.structure.$2.html.tiddlywiki5`, `meta.attribute.$1.html.tiddlywiki5`, which is VS Code's
html grammar's own spelling carried through. The realized `meta` vocabulary runs UNBOUNDED and the
static count of 194 understates it, exactly as html's 62 static names understate its 53 generated
ones.

!! What the house should take and decline

* ''TAKE:'' `meta.*` painting 0/65 stands as the discipline working. Do not move a `meta` name to a
  styled root to "buy reach" — that is the `markup.other`-to-`keyword.control.list` trade the house
  already refused.
* ''TAKE:'' the interpolated `meta` names stand within measured first-party practice and want no
  change. `meta.tag.widget.$3` and `meta.variable.call.parameter.tw-$1` are html's own shape.
* ''DECLINE:'' interpolating into a STYLED root. Vue does it; the risk is that an unrecognised
  `entity.name.tag.$1` mis-colours where an unrecognised `meta.…$1` cannot colour at all. The safety
  of an open vocabulary IS the unstyled root.
* ''ONE CONSTRAINT TO HONOUR'' — CLAIMED BY SOURCE, Sublime: //"`meta.function.php
  meta.function.parameters.php` should never occur"// and //"there should never be more than one
  `meta.annotation*` scope on the stack at any given time."// That bites STACKING two variants of one
  meta family, not interpolation. Worth a gate; no evidence gathered here says this grammar breaks it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #q5-vocabulary-size>>

! 5 — VOCABULARY SIZE AND PUNCTUATION SHARE · OVERTURNED — both readings sit in the ordinary band

''No golden principle names a token count. Measured against grammars that can be counted, 551 names
reads LARGE-ORDINARY, a 26% punctuation share of the vocabulary reads MIDDLING, and a 52% punctuation
share of emitted tokens reads NORMAL for a link-dense markup corpus.''

!! Vocabulary — MEASURED ABROAD, derived by walking every `name`/`contentName`

|!grammar |!bytes |!distinct scopes |!`punctuation.*` |!`meta.*` |!punct. share |!interpolated |
|VS Code C++ |677,832 |471 |150 |103 |''31.8%'' |54 |
|''this grammar'' (all of `syntaxes/`) |— |''551'' |''145'' |''194'' |''26.3%'' |''54'' |
|VS Code TypeScript |231,555 |261 |44 |60 |16.9% |0 |
|VS Code LaTeX |108,756 |142 |33 |43 |23.2% |1 |
|VS Code markdown |79,877 |135 |27 |74 |20.0% |1 |
|MediaWiki wikitext |— |125 |18 |65 |14.4% |0 |
|Org |— |121 |3 |100 |2.5% |0 |
|AsciiDoc (hand-authored base) |57,091 |103 |11 |1 |10.7% |0 |
|VS Code html |84,416 |62 |14 |22 |22.6% |''53'' |
|Vue |33,119 |59 |14 |10 |23.7% |4 |
|DokuWiki |— |44 |17 |8 |38.6% |0 |
|Creole |— |31 |9 |9 |29.0% |0 |
|reStructuredText |— |25 |''0'' |''0'' |0.0% |0 |

Read three things off it.

# ''551 exceeds every grammar measured, C++ included.'' It reads large. But html's 62 STATIC names
  understate it badly — 53 of its names generate from captures — and this grammar's 54 interpolated
  templates do the same to its 551. Neither count names a realized vocabulary.
# ''The punctuation SHARE sits mid-band.'' 26.3% falls between html's 22.6% and C++'s 31.8%, and
  below DokuWiki's 38.6%. It reads unremarkable.
# ''The span of practice runs 0% to 38.6%.'' reStructuredText ships ZERO punctuation scopes and Org
  ships three. Both fold delimiters into content — and reStructuredText pays for it in a way this
  house already has a name for (see §6).

!! Token share — MEASURED ABROAD, actual `vscode-textmate` tokenization

A token counts as punctuation where ANY scope on its stack opens `punctuation.`.

|!corpus |!grammar |!tokens |!punct., all |!punct., non-whitespace |
|VS Code `README.md`, link-dense prose |markdown |459 |''49.7%'' |''57.1%'' |
|`introduction.html` from the WHATWG spec |html |16,537 |''45.4%'' |''51.8%'' |
|CommonMark `spec.txt`, code-block heavy |markdown |15,548 |23.0% |30.4% |

''COLLIDE THE INSTRUMENT FIRST.'' The abroad figures count a token as punctuation where ANY scope on
its stack opens `punctuation.`; the council's 52.2% counted the DEEPEST scope. Re-measured here on
the abroad criterion, over `corpus/wikitext` through `tools/tokenizer.js`:

```
tokens 3447 · punctuation-carrying 1519 · 44.1% of tokens · 14.9% of characters
```

The character figure reproduces the council's 15.0% exactly, so the instruments agree and only the
criterion differed. ''On a like-for-like reading this grammar emits 44.1% — BELOW html's 45.4% and
below link-dense markdown's 49.7%.'' The council read its own number as a defect. Read against the
population, it reads QUIETER than the two languages it was compared to.

The mechanism, MEASURED ABROAD and worth keeping: in html, `punctuation.definition.tag.begin` and
`punctuation.definition.tag.end` each fire 2,957 times — exactly as often as `entity.name.tag`. ''A
tag name costs two punctuation tokens, structurally.'' A wikitext bracket costs the same. The spread
between 23% and 57% reads as corpus composition, never as grammar health: a fenced code block emits
one fat token per line and dilutes everything.

!! Is there a principle? — CLAIMED BY SOURCE

No reference names a count. What the references name instead is a TEST, and it is the one to apply:

TextMate: //"you should think 'would I want these two elements styled differently?' and if so, they
should probably be put into different root groups."//

Sublime: //"being too specific will result in a color scheme that often only looks good for one or two
syntaxes."//

CodeMirror 6: //"it is not obligatory to always attach the most specific tag possible to an element
— if your grammar can't easily distinguish a certain type of element, it is okay to style it as its
more general variant."//

''The question canon asks is never HOW MANY names, it is WHETHER A READER WOULD WANT THESE TWO PAINTED
APART.'' `ReaderRelations` already asks exactly that question, and it is the instrument that governs
here — not a count.

!! What the house should take and decline

* ''TAKE:'' retire the token-count alarm. A 52% punctuation share stands normal; measure a construct
  against `ReaderRelations`, never against a share.
* ''TAKE:'' where a name fails TextMate's question, retire the NAME, and count the retirements as the
  finding. `punctuation.definition.link.outer` versus `.inner` fails it — MEASURED HERE, both paint
  identically in all 65 themes.
* ''DECLINE:'' any sweep that thins the vocabulary to hit a number. reStructuredText hit 25 names by
  folding delimiters into content and lost the delimiter boundary from its token stream entirely.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #q6-wiki-grammars>>

! 6 — HOW THE WIKI LINEAGE HANDLES BRACKET NOISE · UNDECIDED BY PRACTICE, DECIDED BY DEFECT

''Seven markup grammars, four different answers, no convergence — so no canon governs the choice.
What DOES converge is the failure mode of the fold, and it names a defect family this house already
carries.''

All rows below come from a grammar fetched and TOKENIZED, never from a regex read.

|!grammar |!`[[` one token? |!delimiter scope |!the `\|` separator |!surrounding prose |
|''MediaWiki'' wikitext |yes |`punctuation.definition.tag.link.internal.wikitext`, ONE name for both ends |`keyword.operator.wikitext` — ''not punctuation'' |unscoped |
|''Creole'' |yes |`punctuation.definition.link.creole` |''also punctuation'' — the only grammar that scopes the pipe |unscoped |
|''DokuWiki'' |yes |`punctuation.definition.link.dokuwiki`, ONE name both ends |''not tokenized at all'' — swallowed by `(.*?)` |unscoped |
|''Org'' |yes |`punctuation.definition.string.{begin,end}.org` — borrowed from the STRING family |n/a |unscoped |
|''Markdown'' (VS Code) |`](` = two tokens, two glyphs |`punctuation.*`, and the only grammar splitting begin from end |n/a |`meta.paragraph` |
|''AsciiDoc'' |macro `[` `]` ''not captured'' |inherits `markup.other.url.asciidoc`; bold `*` → flat `punctuation.definition.asciidoc` |n/a |unscoped |
|''reStructuredText'' |''no — swallows the first content character'' |''none — zero `punctuation.*` in the file'' |n/a |unscoped |

!! The three answers, and what each costs

# ''DELIMITER AS PUNCTUATION'' — wikitext, Creole, DokuWiki, Markdown, Org. The majority.
# ''DELIMITER FOLDED INTO CONTENT'' — AsciiDoc's macro brackets carry no capture at all, so they
  inherit `markup.other.url.asciidoc` from the rule's own `name`. ''This is F5's fold, shipped.'' And
  note HOW it is done: not by a compound root, but by declining to capture, so the CONTAINER's family
  reaches the glyph.
# ''NO DELIMITER SCOPE ANYWHERE'' — reStructuredText, 25 names, zero `punctuation.*`.

!! The failure the fold produces — MEASURED ABROAD

reStructuredText's `begin: "(?<=[\\s\"'(\\[{<]|^)\\*{2}[^\\s*]"` swallows the first content character
into the opening delimiter. Tokenized on `**bold**`:

```
"**b"   markup.bold      <- delimiter and first letter are ONE token
"old"   markup.bold
"**"    markup.bold
```

''That is the SWALLOW family, in a shipped grammar.'' No consumer can recover the delimiter boundary
from the token stream. `project_memetic_wikitext_vscode_grammar` already names SWALLOW and INVENTION
as the only two defect families, and the fold's cheapest spelling — widening a `begin` until the
delimiter stops being its own token — lands directly in one of them.

Creole's oddity earns its own note, because it is the exact shape of this grammar's compound-root
names: `invalid.illegal.punctuation.link.creole`, with `punctuation` in the THIRD segment, where no
`punctuation.*` selector reaches it.

!! What the house should take and decline

* ''TAKE:'' the mechanism AsciiDoc uses, never the mechanism F5 named. Fold by strengthening the
  CONTAINER's name and letting the glyph inherit it, or by pairing scopes on the glyph. Both keep the
  delimiter its own token.
* ''TAKE:'' that this grammar already stands on the majority answer, and the wiki lineage carries no
  convergent rule to correct it toward.
* ''DECLINE:'' folding by widening a `begin` to swallow the delimiter. reStructuredText measures what
  that costs, and a house that names SWALLOW as a defect family cannot ship one on purpose.
* ''DECLINE:'' Org's borrowed `punctuation.definition.string.*` on a link bracket. It reaches themes
  well and names the wrong thing, which is the trade this house already refused at
  `keyword.control.list`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #q7-one-construct-one-colour>>

! 7 — "ONE CONSTRUCT, ONE COLOUR" · UNDECIDED — no canon states it, and the better instrument already stands here

''No naming reference states a colours-per-construct principle. What canon states instead is a
QUESTION about pairs, and this house already encodes that question as `ReaderRelations`. The instinct
wants a bound; the canon offers a judgement, and a bound would be the weaker instrument.''

!! What the references actually say

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — TextMate, and it is the closest thing to a governing sentence:
//"instead of putting everything below `keyword` (as your formal language definition may insist) you
should think 'would I want these two elements styled differently?' and if so, they should probably be
put into different root groups."//

That asks about ''a pair'', never about a total. It licenses four colours in one construct exactly
where four pairwise judgements each answer yes.

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — Sublime supplies the counter-pressure, also pairwise:
//"being too specific will result in a color scheme that often only looks good for one or two
syntaxes."//

CLAIMED BY SOURCE — CodeMirror 6 supplies the release valve:
//"it is not obligatory to always attach the most specific tag possible to an element … it is okay to
style it as its more general variant."//

Three vendors, three pairwise tests, no count.

!! The reading that settles the alarm — MEASURED HERE, reproduced

Colours a reader meets inside one construct, over 65 themes, whitespace trimmed:

|!construct |!median |!max |!tokens |
|HTML control `<div class="box" id="top">` |4 |6 |15 |
|TW5 widget `<$list filter="x" variable="i"/>` |4 |5 |16 |
|TW5 call `<<greeting name="world" tone="warm">>` |4 |6 |15 |
|TW5 image `[img width=32 [alt\|pic.png]]` |5 |6 |13 |
|TW5 pretty link `[[Link Text\|Target]]` |2 |3 |7 |

''A reader opening a `.tid` beside an `.html` meets the same loudness.'' Whatever standard governs
here already governs HTML, and no theme author has asked HTML to be quieter. The image reads one
louder than HTML and stands as the single construct a bound would bite.

!! Where the rhyme is worth having, and where it reads merely suggestive

* ''`ReaderRelations` is the instrument, and it already outranks a count.'' It holds three relations —
  `together`, `alike`, `apart` — each carrying a SPECIMEN, the WORDS inside it, and a `why`. A
  colours-per-construct bound would answer a question `together` already answers better, because
  `together` names WHICH parts must agree rather than HOW MANY may differ.
* ''The one gap worth filling'' — MEASURED HERE, `together` carries ONE entry, on link text. The call
  and the widget spell one idea and no `apart` or `alike` entry governs their argument names; §3's
  finding wants a relation, not a bound.
* ''A real rhyme, sourced, and it points somewhere NEITHER side proposed.'' Bringhurst, //The Elements
  of Typographic Style// §3.5.2 "Don't clutter the foreground", verbatim: //"When boldface is used to
  emphasize words, it is usually best to leave the punctuation in the background, which is to say, in
  the basic text font. It is the words, not the punctuation, that merit emphasis."// And the criterion
  he names: //"minimizing the surface disturbance on the page."// The rule §3.5.1 above it reads
  //"Change one parameter at a time."//

  Read against this grammar: BACKGROUND means the prose treatment, not a third colour and not the
  construct's own emphasis. That declines the fold (which hands the delimiter the content's emphasis)
  AND declines a loud punctuation colour, and leaves a third option nobody put on the table —
  ''a delimiter that reads like prose''. AsciiDoc's uncaptured macro brackets and reStructuredText's
  zero punctuation scopes both land there, and MEASURED HERE `punctuation.definition.operand.begin`
  standing alone paints in 34 of 65 themes, which means it already reads as prose in 31.

  Mark the weight honestly: a 1992 rule about ink on paper, arriving at the same place as a 2024
  colorscheme, rhymes — it does not rule.
* ''The sharpest empirical result found, and it governs the outer/inner split directly.'' Sarkar's
  survey of prior work, quoting Green &amp; Anderson 1956 and Rubin 1988: //"colouring tokens in text has
  no effect on the speed of visual search when the colour of the target is unknown, but when the
  colour of the target is known, search times are considerably smaller."// A distinction a reader
  cannot predict buys nothing measurable. And Gilmore &amp; Green 1988, per the same survey: //"the added
  information is only effective if it relates to the task."//
* ''The colour-count ceiling, now primary.'' Ware, //Information Visualization: Perception for
  Design//, Ch. 4: //"We can only use between six and twelve color codes reliably. This results partly
  from contrast effects that can alter the appearance of colors."// The constructs here read a median
  of 4 and a maximum of 6 — inside the ceiling, at its lower edge. ''Do not attach Miller's 7±2 to
  this number'': Ware names contrast effects and colour-category confusion as the mechanism, never
  working-memory span, and the wrong mechanism attached to a right number is an instrument lie.
* ''The budget argument, sourced.'' Butterick, //Practical Typography//: //"if everything is
  emphasized, then nothing is emphasized … It also gives you nowhere to go when you need to emphasize
  a word."// That is the reason to keep a construct quiet — not tidiness, but reserve.
* ''NOT SOURCED, and named so nobody re-walks it:'' Lupton's hierarchy sentence (unverified);
  Hannebauer et al. 2018 (the publisher elided the abstract; the no-benefit finding stands as search
  summary only); Beelders &amp; du Plessis (nothing found); Gestalt grouping applied to syntax
  highlighting (no published link exists — that bridge reads as inference, never citation); Tufte
  (not searched — do not cite Tufte from this work).

!! What the house should take and decline

* ''TAKE:'' the frame "as loud as HTML" as the measured answer to "too loud". It reproduces, and it
  is the comparison a reader actually makes.
* ''TAKE:'' a `together` entry for the call-and-widget argument name, so §3's recommendation answers
  to the judgement seat rather than to a preference.
* ''TAKE, and it is the one genuinely new direction in this section:'' Bringhurst's background rule
  as the tie-breaker the house lacks. Where `ReaderRelations` returns no judgement on a delimiter,
  the delimiter reads as PROSE — neither folded into the construct nor given a colour of its own.
  That is a rule a gate can hold and a reader can predict, and Green &amp; Anderson say predictability is
  where colour buys anything at all.
* ''DECLINE:'' a colours-per-construct gate. No reference states one, the number varies with theme
  (median 4, max 6), and a bound would rule the healthy HTML case broken — the same shape as the gate
  that would have ruled a healthy grammar broken by measuring container scopes alone.
* ''DECLINE:'' Miller's 7±2 beside Ware's 6-12. Right number, wrong mechanism.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #q8-the-calm-lever>>

! 8 — THE CALM-THEME LEVER · LEGAL, UNPOLICED, AND STILL THE WRONG LEVER

''`contributes.configurationDefaults` carrying `editor.tokenColorCustomizations` WORKS — traced
through VS Code's own validation, with 450 shipped precedents. It also cannot be scoped to a
language, deep-merges into the user's own settings so they cannot cleanly remove it, and crosses a
line the vendor's own twenty-four bundled extensions never cross. Three safer levers exist, and two
of them cost nothing.''

!! It passes both gates — TRACED, not assumed

MEASURED ABROAD, `src/vs/workbench/api/common/configurationExtensionPoint.ts` lines 212-231:

```ts
const allowedScopes = [ConfigurationScope.MACHINE_OVERRIDABLE, ConfigurationScope.WINDOW,
                       ConfigurationScope.RESOURCE, ConfigurationScope.LANGUAGE_OVERRIDABLE];
if (registeredPropertyScheme?.disallowConfigurationDefault) { … delete overrides[key]; continue; }
if (registeredPropertyScheme?.scope && !allowedScopes.includes(registeredPropertyScheme.scope)) {
    … "Only defaults for machine-overridable, window, resource and language overridable scoped
    settings are supported." … delete overrides[key]; continue; }
```

`src/vs/workbench/services/themes/common/themeConfiguration.ts` registers
`editor.tokenColorCustomizations` with NO `scope` and NO `disallowConfigurationDefault`;
`configurationRegistry.ts:804` defaults an unset scope to `ConfigurationScope.WINDOW`. WINDOW sits in
`allowedScopes`. ''It registers, raises no warning, and takes effect.''

The gate proves itself real on a neighbour: `telemetry.telemetryLevel` carries APPLICATION scope and
gets rejected — VS Code's configuration owner, issue #245787: //"This is not supported because it is
an application wide setting therefore cannot be overridden by an extension."// And a global,
non-`[language]` default stands officially supported, issue #181271: //"It is supported."//

MEASURED ABROAD — the contribution-point doc carries NO restriction language at all. Thirty lines,
one `files.autoSave` example and one `[markdown]` example, and nothing about scopes or forbidden
settings. ''The docs do not answer this question; the source does.''

!! Three costs, each traced

# ''It can NEVER be scoped to a language.'' `configurationRegistry.ts` `updateSchema()` admits only
  LANGUAGE_OVERRIDABLE settings into `resourceLanguageSettingsSchema`, and a WINDOW-scoped setting
  never enters. VS Code's themes owner closed the request for exactly this, verbatim:
  //"We don't support defining tokenColors by language, neither in themes nor in settings.
  → `editor.tokenColorCustomizations` can not be scoped by language."// and //"Instead you have to
  write language specific rules."// ''The ONLY fence available is the language-suffixed scope name —
  which this grammar already carries, and which the house ruling already requires.''
# ''It sits INSIDE the user, not under them.'' `configurationModels.ts:219` `mergeContents` DEEP-MERGES
  defaults with user settings for object-valued settings. A user writing their own
  `editor.tokenColorCustomizations` with one key does not clear a contributed `textMateRules` array;
  removing it wants an explicit `"textMateRules": []`, which destroys their own rules too.
# ''No hardcoded colour survives contact with an arbitrary theme.'' Same maintainer, same issue:
  //"its impossible to pick colors and styles that work in all themes. Theming has to be the
  responsibility of the theme or the user."// That is the norm stated plainly — never "forbidden",
  always ''not yours''.

!! The strongest evidence reads as a SILENCE — MEASURED ABROAD

VS Code ships twenty-four bundled extensions using `configurationDefaults`. Four fetched; ''ZERO set
any colour.'' Every one behavioural and `[language]`-scoped:

```
"[markdown]":      { "editor.unicodeHighlight.ambiguousCharacters": false, … }
"[search-result]": { "editor.lineNumbers": "off" }
"[git-commit]":    { "editor.rulers": [50, 72], "editor.wordWrap": "off", … }
"[python]":        { "diffEditor.ignoreTrimWhitespace": false, … }
```

''House style at the vendor: BEHAVIOUR yes, COLOUR never.'' 450 third-party extensions depart from
it; the vendor does not. And no marketplace or publishing guidance forbids it — the Settings UX
Do/Don't list names nothing about colour, themes, or overriding a user's configuration. ''There stands
no policy to violate and no norm to hide behind.''

!! What this repository already contributes — MEASURED HERE

```json
"contributes": { "languages": …, "grammars": …, "snippets": …,
  "configurationDefaults": { "files.associations": {
    "*.tiddlywiki5": "tiddlywiki5", "*.tw": "tiddlywiki5", … "*.mem": "memetic-wikitext" } } }
```

''Behavioural, and claiming only file extensions this extension itself defines.'' It sits on the
vendor's side of the line. A token-colour default would move it across, and would move it to a
setting that ''cannot be narrowed to this language''.

`tools/ships-no-runtime.test.js` already names the shape of the decision: its DECLARATIVE allowlist
carries `configurationDefaults`, `configuration`, `themes`, `semanticTokenScopes` — and its first
assertion reads `pkg.main === undefined` and `pkg.activationEvents === undefined`.

!! The three levers the ecosystem actually ships — MEASURED ABROAD

# ''fontStyle-ONLY rules on vendor-prefixed scopes.'' robotcode ships fifteen rules of which twelve
  carry pure `fontStyle` and three carry a deliberately empty `"settings": {}` — an explicit
  un-styling. koka, tds-vscode and vdm-vscode all target language-suffixed or vendor-prefixed scopes.
  ''A `fontStyle` carries no colour, so aeschli's objection does not reach it'' — italic and bold
  survive contact with any theme in a way a hex value cannot.
# ''A theme-scoped README snippet the user pastes.'' sveltejs/language-tools:
  //"If your syntax highlighting seems to be not working for Svelte components or you feel that some
  colors are wrong, you can add something like the following to your `settings.json`"// — wrapped in
  `"[<Name of your theme>]"`. ''That wrapper is exactly what a contributed default cannot
  responsibly use, because an extension does not know the reader's theme.'' Zero risk, zero
  stickiness, and it degrades when the reader switches themes.
# ''Custom semantic token types with a scope-map fallback and ZERO colour opinion.'' rust-analyzer's
  whole `configurationDefaults` reads `[rust]` quick-suggestions and a file-nesting pattern. Instead
  it declares 38 custom semantic token types and 17 modifiers, fifteen of them punctuation subtypes:
  `{ "id": "brace", "description": "Style for { or }", "superType": "punctuation" }` — and hands the
  colour decision to the theme via `contributes.semanticTokenScopes`.

!! And rust-analyzer already SHIPS the dial F6 says cannot exist

MEASURED ABROAD, from its `contributes.configuration`:

```
rust-analyzer.semanticHighlighting.punctuation.enable                 default = FALSE
rust-analyzer.semanticHighlighting.punctuation.specialization.enable  default = FALSE
rust-analyzer.semanticHighlighting.punctuation.separate.macro.bang    default = FALSE
rust-analyzer.semanticHighlighting.operator.specialization.enable     default = FALSE
```

//"Use semantic tokens for punctuation. When disabled, rust-analyzer will emit semantic tokens only
for punctuation tokens when they are tagged with modifiers or have a special role."//

''A three-position loudness dial for punctuation, shipped, DEFAULTING TO OFF, flippable without a
reload'' — `onDidChangeSemanticTokens` in `vscode.d.ts` and `workspace/semanticTokens/refresh` in the
LSP spec both re-request tokens live. F6 stands CORRECT that a TextMate grammar carries no dial, and
INCOMPLETE in stopping there: the dial exists, one layer up, and someone already built it.

''The price, stated plainly:'' a provider needs `main` and `activationEvents`, and
`tools/ships-no-runtime.test.js` forbids both. ''Whoever proposes the dial proposes retiring that
gate.''

!! What the house should take and decline

* ''TAKE, at zero cost:'' a README section carrying a theme-scoped
  `editor.tokenColorCustomizations` snippet naming this grammar's own scopes. Svelte's shape exactly.
  Nothing ships, nothing sticks, and the reader keeps the decision.
* ''TAKE, if a default must ship at all:'' `fontStyle` only, never `foreground`, on names already
  carrying the `.tiddlywiki5` suffix. Twelve of robotcode's fifteen rules measure the discipline, and
  a fontStyle carries no colour opinion to be wrong about.
* ''DECLINE a contributed `foreground`.'' It cannot be narrowed to this language, it deep-merges into
  the reader's settings, no colour survives every theme, and the vendor's own twenty-four extensions
  decline it unanimously. ''450 precedents measure what is possible, never what is right.''
* ''DECLINE a contributed THEME as the answer to loudness.'' The gate permits it and a reader opting
  into a whole palette to quieten one language trades far more than they asked to trade.
* ''UNDECIDED, and it wants an operator ruling:'' whether a semantic-token provider — and therefore
  the retirement of `ships-no-runtime` — buys enough. rust-analyzer proves the shape works. This
  extension's whole published identity rests on shipping nothing that runs.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #q9-pattern-rhymes>>

! 9 — PATTERN-INTEGRITY RHYMES · MIXED — one rhyme VINDICATES F3 exactly, one candidate OVERTURNS

Only rhymes carrying a source appear below. Anything judged merely suggestive says so in its own line.

!! RHYME 1 — tree-sitter's bracket/delimiter split, and what themes do with it · VINDICATES F3

CLAIMED BY SOURCE, tree-sitter's own capture vocabulary already stands in this house at
`coordinator-direct.mem:195`:

```
@punctuation.delimiter          delimiters (e.g. `;`, `.`, `,`)
@punctuation.bracket            brackets (e.g. `()`, `{}`, `[]`)
@punctuation.special            special symbols (e.g. `{}` in string interpolation)
```

Three captures, a real distinction, documented. And Neovim's OWN default highlight table, same file at
line 319, collapses all three:

```lua
hi('@punctuation', { link = 'Delimiter' })
```

''One link, no sub-entries.'' The distinction reaches the query author and the tooling; it does not
reach the eye unless a colorscheme goes out of its way. That is F3's finding arriving from a
completely unrelated system: ''a delimiter taxonomy is a machine-facing vocabulary by default.''

F3 is therefore VINDICATED in its reading and OVERTURNED in its alarm. Carrying two names on `[[` is
not an error because the eye cannot see it; it is an error only because ''no grammar carries them as
two TOKENS'' (§1). Keep the vocabulary, fuse the tokens.

!! RHYME 2 — LSP semantic-token MODIFIERS as the dial TextMate lacks · OVERTURNED by the spec's own list

CLAIMED BY SOURCE, LSP 3.17, the complete modifier set:

```
declaration, definition, readonly, static, deprecated, abstract, async,
modification, documentation, defaultLibrary
```

''Every one names a KIND, not an INTENSITY.'' There is no `emphasis`, no `subdued`, no dial. And the
23 token types carry `operator` but NO punctuation type at all — so the construct this question is
about cannot be expressed as a semantic token in the first place.

What semantic tokens genuinely supply is a different axis, and the house already recorded it:
modifiers apply ACROSS types, where a dotted TextMate name cannot. That answers the
macro-definition-versus-macro-call problem. It does not answer loudness.

CLAIMED BY SOURCE, VS Code's own statement of the layering: //"The editor applies the highlighting
from semantic tokens on top of the highlighting from grammars."// The grammar stays the floor and must
read correctly alone — so a semantic layer could never make a loud grammar quiet; it can only paint
over it.

''And a standing house gate forecloses it anyway.'' `tools/ships-no-runtime.test.js` asserts
`pkg.main === undefined` and `pkg.activationEvents === undefined`. A `SemanticTokensProvider` needs
both. `semanticTokenScopes` and `semanticTokenTypes` sit in that gate's DECLARATIVE allowlist and cost
nothing; a PROVIDER turns this extension into one that runs code. ''Whoever proposes the semantic
lever proposes retiring that gate, and should say so.''

!! RHYME 3 — the typographic background rule · VINDICATES the delimiter question, against BOTH proposals

Carried in full at §7. In one line: Bringhurst §3.5.2 rules that punctuation stays in the BASIC TEXT
FONT while the words carry the emphasis, with //"minimizing the surface disturbance"// as the
criterion. A 1992 rule about ink, three shipped 2024 colorschemes painting punctuation dimmer than
body text, and a language server shipping `punctuation.enable: false` arrive at the same place from
three unrelated directions. That convergence is the strongest rhyme in this survey.

!! RHYME 4 — colour buys search speed only when the target colour is KNOWN · SUPPORTS retiring a name

CLAIMED BY SOURCE, via Sarkar's survey of Green &amp; Anderson 1956 and Rubin 1988: //"colouring tokens
in text has no effect on the speed of visual search when the colour of the target is unknown, but when
the colour of the target is known, search times are considerably smaller."//

''A distinction a reader cannot predict buys nothing that has ever been measured.'' That is the
empirical case for retiring `outer` versus `inner`, and the case for NOT inventing a new distinction
merely because a name is available.

!! RHYME 5 — the emphasis budget · SUPPORTS the quiet default

CLAIMED BY SOURCE, Butterick: //"if everything is emphasized, then nothing is emphasized … It also
gives you nowhere to go when you need to emphasize a word."// And on headings, //"Fewer levels, subtler
emphasis"//: //"writers often use too many levels of headings. This leads to increasingly desperate
attempts to make them visually distinct."//

This is the API-design "make the common case quiet" instinct, correctly sourced — and it belongs to
typography, not to API design. ''Do not cite the Principle of Least Astonishment for it''; that
principle governs behaviour matching expectation, never restraint.

!! The colour ceiling, and the mechanism NOT to attach to it

CLAIMED BY SOURCE, Ware, //Information Visualization: Perception for Design//, Ch. 4: //"We can only
use between six and twelve color codes reliably. This results partly from contrast effects that can
alter the appearance of colors."// Independently, ColorBrewer's categorical maxima measure 8 to 12.
Two methods, one ceiling.

MEASURED HERE, the constructs read a median of 4 colours and a maximum of 6 — inside the ceiling, at
its lower edge. ''Miller's 7±2 must stay out of any citation of this'': Ware names contrast effects
and colour-category confusion as the mechanism, never working-memory span, and a right number carrying
a wrong mechanism is exactly the instrument lie this house counts.

!! What this section does NOT establish — named so nobody re-walks it

Gestalt grouping applied to syntax highlighting: no published link found, in two hands. Lupton:
unverified. Hannebauer et al. 2018: the publisher elided the abstract, so the no-benefit finding
stands as a search summary and nothing firmer. Beelders &amp; du Plessis: nothing found. Tufte: not
searched — do not cite Tufte from this work.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #recommendations>>

! RECOMMENDATIONS — ranked by payoff over risk, each naming what must go RED first

Nothing below ships on this reading alone. Each row names the gate that proves it, and a
recommendation whose red nobody can write does not belong on the list.

!! 1 — FIX THE TWO WEAK CONTAINERS. Highest payoff, lowest risk, and the house already ruled it once.

MEASURED HERE. A construct's `begin`/`end` `name` is the family every glyph inside it falls back to
when no deeper rule reaches. Two containers name almost nothing:

|!construct |!container today |!paints |!brackets read the body's colour |
|pretty link |`markup.link.tiddlywiki5` |''8/65'' |5/65 |
|pretty link |`markup.underline.link.tiddlywiki5` |''48/65'' |''29/65'' |
|image |`markup.other.image.tiddlywiki5` |''2/65'' |0/65 |
|image |`markup.underline.link.image.tiddlywiki5` |''48/65'' |''29/65'' |

''This buys the whole delimiter-reads-with-content effect F5 wanted, without touching one punctuation
name, without severing one chain, and without inventing anything.'' AsciiDoc reaches the same effect
the same way — its macro brackets carry no capture at all and inherit the rule's own `name`.

It also dissolves F5's stated caution: the image reads near-invisible because
`markup.other.image` paints 2/65, and `docs/scope-naming-prior-art.mem` recommendation 1 already
named `markup.other.*` a second `meta.*` trap on 2026-09-01. ''The council re-derived a hard answer to
a problem a standing unlanded recommendation dissolves.''

RED FIRST: `tools/colour-witness.js` on a NEW `together` relation — a pretty link's brackets and its
caption read one colour — failing at 5/65, and `theme-paint` on `markup.link.tiddlywiki5` reporting
8/65 beside `markup.underline.link.tiddlywiki5` at 48/65. Also stage a control: the same relation on
the WIDGET, which must not move.

!! 2 — FUSE `[[` AND `]]`, AND THE IMAGE'S TWO BRACKETS. Cheap, canon-backed, and it retires a name.

MEASURED HERE both halves paint identically in all 65 themes, so no reader loses anything. MEASURED
ABROAD, twenty grammars, ''none'' splits a repeated glyph pair. The call already fuses `<<` and `>>`
and carries a PAIRED name on the single token — this grammar's own better answer, two rules away.

Green &amp; Anderson supply the empirical case: a distinction a reader cannot predict buys no measurable
search speed.

RED FIRST: a snapshot assertion that `[[` emits ONE token, written before the change and failing on
today's two. And a control the change must NOT move — the widget's `<` and `$`, which stand as two
GLYPHS and must stay two tokens.

!! 3 — UNIFY THE ARGUMENT NAME, KEEP THE `=` APART. Medium payoff, and it corrects a live misreading.

MEASURED HERE the call's and widget's argument names read the SAME colour in ''6 of 65'' themes — one
idea painted two ways. MEASURED ABROAD the surface rule governs, and the closest analogue in the
whole corpus, MediaWiki's `{{Template|name=value}}`, takes `entity.other.attribute-name` for the name.
Handlebars supplies the hedge if the precise name must survive: `variable.parameter` stacked inside
`entity.other.attribute-name`.

''And the `=` stays apart.'' MEASURED HERE against the checkout, a call's binder carries
`assignmentOperator` in the parse tree and may spell `:`; a widget's carries nothing and admits only
`=`. One names a choice, the other names structure, and the families already say so.

RED FIRST: an `apart` or `together` entry in `ReaderRelations` for the two argument names, failing at
6/65. The judgement seat rules this, never a measurement — so the relation must land before the
grammar moves. `tools/call-parity.test.js` stands as the control: no call-side name may lose its call
scope.

!! 4 — A README SNIPPET FOR THE READER WHO WANTS IT QUIETER. Zero risk, zero stickiness.

Svelte's shape: a `"[<Name of your theme>]"`-wrapped `editor.tokenColorCustomizations` block naming
this grammar's own `.tiddlywiki5` scopes. Nothing ships, nothing merges into the reader's settings,
and it degrades honestly when they switch themes.

RED FIRST: nothing — no gate governs a README, which is precisely why it ranks here rather than
higher. If it wants a gate, the honest one asks that every scope the snippet names still exists in
`syntaxes/`, which `tools/grammar-scopes.js` can answer.

!! 5 — A `meta` STACKING GATE. Small payoff, and it closes an unnamed exposure.

CLAIMED BY SOURCE, Sublime: //"`meta.function.php meta.function.parameters.php` should never occur"//
and //"there should never be more than one `meta.annotation*` scope on the stack at any given time."//

MEASURED HERE, tokenized over `corpus/wikitext`: 245 of 3447 tokens (7.1%) carry more than one `meta`
name sharing a three-segment prefix. `meta.variable.call` reads 68 and `meta.variable.pragma` 65 —
the WIDEN ruling's pairs plus a parameters nesting, three levels deep on one stack.

''COLLIDE THIS INSTRUMENT BEFORE BELIEVING IT.'' The measure conflates a genuine breach with
legitimate NESTING — `meta.element.structure` reads 105 and a div inside a div is not a defect. It
costs no colour either way, since `meta` never paints. What it costs is SELECTOR PRECISION, and this
grammar already leans on injection selectors: `tw5-substitution-injection.json` fires on
`meta.variable.macro.body` alone.

RED FIRST: an instrument that distinguishes a stacked VARIANT from a nested INSTANCE, and fails on
the former only. Until somebody writes that, this row names an exposure and not a defect.

!! 6 — THE SIX SOLE-COMPOUND OPERAND NAMES. Genuinely undecided; do not ship on this reading.

MEASURED HERE the conversion costs the delimiter-joins-content reading in one to four themes per kind
and buys a `punctuation.definition` surface nobody has asked for.
`corpus/legibility-floor.txt:27` already weighed the pair question and found nothing moved.

What that floor did NOT see, MEASURED HERE: the shape stands at six names across THREE roots, not the
one `string.` name it records. Whatever the house rules, it rules on six.

RED FIRST: a reader asking for `punctuation.definition.operand` in their own settings. Absent that,
the correct move reads ''record the finding, widen the floor's note to all six, ship nothing.''

!! WHAT THIS READING DECLINES OUTRIGHT

* ''A colours-per-construct gate.'' No reference states one; it would rule the healthy HTML case broken.
* ''A vocabulary-size target.'' reStructuredText hit 25 names by folding delimiters into content and
  lost the delimiter boundary from its token stream.
* ''A contributed `foreground` default.'' Legal, unpoliced, 450 precedents — and it cannot be narrowed
  to this language, it deep-merges into the reader's settings, and the vendor's own twenty-four
  bundled extensions decline it unanimously.
* ''Folding by widening a `begin`.'' reStructuredText measures the cost, and it lands in the SWALLOW
  family this house already names.
* ''Miller's 7±2 beside Ware's colour ceiling.'' Right number, wrong mechanism.

